  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Areas of valley fog/stratus into early next week. \n\n-  Scattered showers and gusty winds for Sunday. \n\n-  High pressure the middle of next week bringing more valley\n   inversions, fog/stratus potential. \n\n\nToday through Sunday Night:\n\nA weak disturbance traversing the Northern Rockies this morning \nwill trigger some light showers across northwest Montana. Thus \nfar, considerable mid- to high-level cloud cover has largely \nsuppressed fog and low stratus formation. However, localized \npatches of fog and stratus will likely redevelop in areas that \nexperience partial clearing.\n\nBy this afternoon, the development of southerly winds will promote mixing\nin several valleys, leading to significant warming, particularly \nacross portions of north-central Idaho, Lemhi County, southwest \nMontana, and the Bitterroot and Mission valleys. Conversely, the \nMissoula Valley may struggle to mix out. If the inversion holds, \ntemperatures will remain notably cooler, though maximum heating \nwill heavily depend on the extent of any lingering fog or stratus.\n\nOn Sunday, a cold front will sweep across the region, generating scattered\nshowers primarily over northwest Montana and north-central Idaho.\nThe most significant impact from this frontal passage will be the\ncomplete scouring of persistent valley inversions. This front \nwill also usher in gusty winds, with widespread surface gusts of \n20 to 30 mph expected by Sunday afternoon. Higher terrain along \nthe Continental Divide, particularly near Glacier National Park, \ncan expect gusts exceeding 40 mph.\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nA secondary, subtle disturbance is progged to track through the Northern\nRockies late Monday into Tuesday. This system will maintain enough\natmospheric mixing to inhibit strong valley inversions while reintroducing\na chance for light showers, again favoring northwest Montana.\n\nBy Wednesday, long-range guidance strongly favors the building of a\nhigh-amplitude upper-level ridge over the region, dominating the weather\npattern through the remainder of the workweek. This quiescent pattern\ncarries a high probability for the redevelopment of strong, persistent\nvalley inversions. Consequently, areas of valley fog and low \nstratus are anticipated, which will moderate diurnal heating and \nkeep valley temperatures below their full mixed potential.",
